\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% 

\subsection{Type 1 Diabetes \& Islet Autoimmunity}
\label{sec:ia}
Type 1 Diabetes (T1D) is a complex autoimmune disorder where the immune system erroneously targets and destroys insulin-producing pancreatic islet-$\beta$ cells, leading to a lack of insulin and elevated blood glucose levels\cite{katsarouType1Diabetes2017}. Unlike \textit{monogenic} diseases -- like sickle cell disease or cystic fibrosis, which result from variation within a single gene and are relatively rare -- \textit{complex} diseases are influenced by multiple chromosomal regions containing genetic variation, known as loci \cite{MonogenicDisorderOverview}. The pathogenesis of T1D is believed to follow a continuous progression, beginning with the appearance of islet autoantibodies, followed by $\beta$-cell destruction, the onset of dysglycemia, and ultimately, the manifestation of hyperglycemia-related symptoms. Its preclinical stage, known as Islet Autoimmunity (IA), is characterized by the presence of autoantibodies prior to clinical diagnosis \cite{katsarouType1Diabetes2017,onengut-gumuscuGeneticVariantsComplement2024}. However, the precise onset of IA remains somewhat ambiguous. The definition used by the Diabetes AutoImmunity Study in the Young (DAISY) cohort classifies IA as the presence of one or more islet autoantibodies on two or more consecutive visits, or a single autoantibody-positive visit followed by a Type 1 Diabetes diagnosis at the next visit \cite{frohnertLateonsetIsletAutoimmunity2017}.

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{trialnet.png}
    \caption{{\bf Stages of T1D}\\
    The progression of T1D follows three recognized stages. \textbf{Stage 1} marks the onset of Islet Autoimmunity (IA), with the presence of two or more diabetes-related autoantibodies but normal blood glucose levels. \textbf{Stage 2} is characterized by worsening $\beta$-cell function and emerging dysglycemia, despite the absence of symptoms. \textbf{Stage 3} is the clinical onset of T1D, marked by symptomatic hyperglycemia requiring insulin therapy \cite{Type1Diabetes,onengut-gumuscuGeneticVariantsComplement2024}.}
    \label{fig:stages_t1d}
\end{figure}
\par
Often diagnosed in early childhood, T1D left untreated can result in serious complications such as diabetic ketoacidosis, a life-threatening condition. With the disease incidence rising \cite{atkinsonType1Diabetes2014}, efforts to understand the pathogenesis and etiology of T1D continue with the hopes of phase-specific therapeutic intervention to mitigate life-threatening complications, eliminate the burden of insulin pumps, and improve quality of life.

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{rising-incidence-t1d.png}
    \caption{{\bf Incidence of T1D in children aged 0-14 years} \\
    The incidence of T1D has steadily increased across multiple countries over the past several decades, as shown in this longitudinal dataset\cite{atkinsonType1Diabetes2014}.}
    \label{fig:incidence_t1d}
\end{figure}
\par

T1D is primarily considered a genetic disease, with environmental factors-—such as viral infections—potentially influencing disease progression and the onset of IA. Ongoing research continues to investigate the viral onset hypothesis and the role of the complement system, which is primarily encoded within the Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) region \cite{onengut-gumuscuGeneticVariantsComplement2024}. However, the focus of this research remains on genetic susceptibility.
\par
The HLA region represents the strongest and longest-known genetic risk factor for T1D. The first association between HLA and juvenile-onset T1D was reported in the early 1970s through serological HLA typing \cite{singalHistocompatibilityHLAAntigens1973}. This finding was later validated in the 1980s using targeted genetic association testing, which pinpointed specific HLA-DQB1 alleles as key determinants of T1D susceptibility \cite{toddHLADQBGeneContributes1987}. This highly polymorphic region has been associated with many other autoimmune diseases and constitutes roughly 40\% of the heritability of T1D\cite{onengut-gumuscuGeneticVariantsComplement2024}.
\par
Despite decades of research, questions about the genetic architecture of T1D remain. Over 60 candidate loci, collectively explaining roughly 80\% of the heritability\cite{pangMissingHeritabilityType2022}, have been identified with respect to T1D. However, this leaves approximately 20\% of heritability unexplained, a phenomenon known as the missing heritability problem. Addressing this gap requires further investigation into rare variants, common variants with small effect sizes, and gene-gene interactions (epistasis)\cite{pangMissingHeritabilityType2022,roseEpistasisImmunityRole2012}.
\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{missing-heritability.jpg}
    \caption{{\bf Potential sources of the missing heritability in T1D} \\
    Here G-G interaction is a gene by gene interaction and G-E is gene by environment interaction \cite{pangMissingHeritabilityType2022}.}
    \label{fig:missing_herit}
\end{figure}



\subsection{Epistasis}
\label{sec:epi}
\textit{Epistasis} has been defined and redefined many times since its coinage by Bateson in 1909, often leading to ambiguity in its definition. Seldom mentioned, however, is that Bateson also defined the term \textit{hypostasis} \cite{batesonMendelsPrinciplesHeredity1909,cordellEpistasisWhatIt2002}. These two terms were initially meant to describe a hierarchical relationship where a higher-level factor (epistatic gene/allele) controls whether a lower-level factor (hypostatic gene/allele) can be expressed. Here, \textit{epistatic} and \textit{hypostatic} derive from the Greek prefixes \textit{epi} meaning "upon", \textit{hypo} meaning "under", and root \textit{stasis} meaning "standing". Thus, to Bateson, an epistatic gene "stands upon" a hypostatic gene, masking the hypostatic gene's expression. 
\par
Epistasis, or \textit{dual epistacy}, was redefined under a statistical context by Fisher in 1918 as a deviation from linearity in allelic effects with respect to some quantitative phenotype $D$ \cite{cordellEpistasisWhatIt2002,fisherXVTheCorrelationRelatives1919}. Here, we already see differing definitions of epistasis emerging just 9 years apart, each carrying distinct implications. While Fisherian statistical epistasis does not necessarily imply biological or functional epistasis, it provides a framework for detecting genetic interactions that warrant further investigation. The modern definition of epistasis is largely an umbrella term for genetic interaction. Under this umbrella, we find the term \textit{positive/negative epistasis}, which refers to the interaction between genetic variants where positive (negative) epistasis occurs when their combined effect on a trait or disease risk is greater (less) than the sum of their individual effects. At the population level, this deviation from additivity plays a crucial role in shaping genetic architecture and disease susceptibility \cite{johnsonEpistasisEvolutionRecent2023}.
\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{epistasis.jpg}
    \caption{{\bf Gene-Gene Interaction} \\
    This is a conceptual model showing how Gene-Gene interactions play a role in phenotypic variation through multiple molecular interactions, including DNA-DNA, protein-DNA, protein-RNA, and protein-protein interactions \cite{wangMeaningInteraction2010}.}
    \label{fig:epistasis}
\end{figure}
\par 
Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) are a modern approach to discover genetic associations with a disease. GWAS test millions of representative single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) from a population in a linear framework where each SNP is tested for association with a phenotype individually. The standard correction to account for multiple hypothesis testing in GWAS is the Bonferroni correction, which adjusts for the number of independent SNPs in the genome. This method typically applies a genome-wide significance threshold of $5\times 10^{-8}$ for European populations to identify statistically significant associations and similar but more stringent threshold for African populations \cite{evangelouGeneticEpidemiologyMethods2018,shamStatisticalPowerSignificance2014}.
\par
There are issues with this standard of practice. The standard genome-wide significance threshold faces challenges in evolving GWAS practices. It was developed for common variants, potentially lacking power for rare variants. It does not take into account the prior probability of a variant being associated with a phenotype, nor does it account for the statistical power of the test which both influence the interpretation of the $p$-value. These oversights can distort results, particularly when considering epistasis, where interactions between variants may not align with assumptions of independence and testing SNPs in pairs or tuples of length $n$ drastically increase the already large number of hypotheses. Applying the standard threshold universally risks missing meaningful associations while inflating false positives, especially in diverse populations or complex phenotypes\cite{jiangBayesianMethodEvaluating2011,shamStatisticalPowerSignificance2014}.
\par
Genome-wide association interaction (GWAI) studies are an extension of GWAS aimed at epistasis detection\cite{bessonovCautionaryNoteImpact2015,gusarevaPracticalAspectsGenomewide2014}. There are two categories of methods that emerge when considering GWAI: exhaustive and non-exhaustive\cite{gusarevaPracticalAspectsGenomewide2014}. Non-exhaustive methods avoid the challenge of multiple testing correction by limiting the analysis in some way. Typically, these analyses determine candidate genes a prior to test, use GWAS results to look at interactions between significant SNPs overlooking those SNPs with small effects with potentially large interaction effects, or use some sort of dimensionality reduction\cite{gusarevaPracticalAspectsGenomewide2014,anChallengeDetectingEpistasis2009}. To address the burden of multiple testing in exhaustive approaches, Bayesian methods have been recommended for both GWAS and GWAI\cite{jiangBayesianMethodEvaluating2011,stephensBayesianStatisticalMethods2009,lopezBayesianStatisticalMethods,zhouPolygenicModelingBayesian2013,shamStatisticalPowerSignificance2014}.

\subsection{Bayesian Statistics}
\label{sec:bayes}
Bayesian statistics is named after Reverend Thomas Bayes, a part-time mathematician who was among the first recorded individuals to attempt solving the problem of inverse probability\cite{shoemakerBayesianStatisticsGenetics1999,gelmanBayesianDataAnalysisa}. Inverse probability seeks to determine the probability of a cause given an observed outcome as opposed to classical probability which estimates the likelihood of an event given known conditions. In Bayes' work that was posthumously communicated by his friend and colleague Richard Price, he posed the problem 
\begin{quote}
    "\textit{Given} the number of times in which an unknown event has happened and failed: 
    \textit{Required} the chance that the probability of its happening in a single trial lies 
    somewhere between any two degrees of probability that can be named." \cite{bayesLIIEssaySolving1997}
\end{quote}

\noindent
Bayes' original work did not explicitly introduce what we now know as Bayes' theorem:
$$p(\theta \mid y) = \frac{p(y \mid \theta)p(\theta)}{p(y)}$$
This was instead formulated by Laplace. Bayes' contribution was rather the concept of compromising prior information, $p(\theta)$, with information from observed data, $p(y \mid \theta)$. He introduces this concept with an analogy to a billiard table where he considers a scenario where a ball is randomly thrown onto a unit interval, and the goal is to estimate the probability of a future event occuring based on where the ball landed. Bayes introduced what we would now call a uniform prior distribution on $\theta$ such that $\theta$ was modeled as the probability parameter of a binomial distribution\cite{bayesLIIEssaySolving1997,fienbergWhenDidBayesian2006}. 
\par
In population genetics, the classical frequentist approach to discovering associations with a phenotype typically involves computing the $p$-value for the null hypothesis ($H_0$) of no association. Despite our desire to interpret the $p$-value as the probability of $H_0$ being true (i.e. $p$-value $< 0.05$ implying significance through rejecting the null in favor of the alternative hypothesis $H_1$), this is simply not the case. The $p$-value relies not only on the how unlikely it is under $H_0$ but also how unlikely it is under $H_1$ which differs from study to study due to differences in power and measures the extremeness of the result under the null\cite{lesaffreBayesianBiostatistics2012}. A fundamental limitation of the $p$-value aside from its comparability across studies is that with-in a study, two genetic associations with equivalent $p$-values may have different levels of true association due to underlying factors such as minor allele frequency (MAF) which is the frequency of the non-dominant allele in the reference population. Some of these limitations can be addressed using the Bayesian framework, although doing so comes at the cost of increased modeling assumptions\cite{stephensBayesianStatisticalMethods2009}. 
\par
Modern Bayesian approaches incorporate prior probabilities and probabilistic modeling to improve statistical power and control false positives. In the context of a frequentist GWAS, the number of comparisons needs to be corrected for. The Bayesian perspective avoids this requirement as the $p$-value analogue is the \textit{posterior probability}, $P(H_0 = \text{TRUE} \mid \text{Data})$, and is truly interpreted as a probability of true association conditioned on the observed data\cite{lesaffreBayesianBiostatistics2012}.

\subsection{Motivation}
\label{sec:motiv}
Epistasis is particularly important in complex autoimmune diseases, where interactions between immune-related loci can shape immune system function and the loss of self-tolerance\cite{roseEpistasisImmunityRole2012}. T1D is a prime example of this.
While significant progress has been made in identifying genetic risk loci, including the well-established role of the HLA region, a substantial proportion of heritability remains unexplained\cite{onengut-gumuscuGeneticVariantsComplement2024}. This missing heritability may be partially attributed to epistasis, which is not captured by standard GWAS\cite{pangMissingHeritabilityType2022}.
\par
Traditional GWAS frameworks are optimized for detecting marginal effects of individual SNPs but are not well-suited for modeling the joint effects of interacting loci. When considering epistasis, the number of SNP pairs (or $n$-tuples) to test increases combinatorially, exacerbating the multiple testing problem and further reducing power. Furthermore, $p$-values in the frequentist framework are often misinterpreted, and their interpretation can vary substantially depending on study-specific factors \cite{lesaffreBayesianBiostatistics2012,walakiraDetectingGeneGene2022}.
\par
Bayesian methods offer a compelling alternative. They are not only well-suited for discovery in large-scale genomic studies but also allow for probabilistic modeling of uncertainty and incorporation of prior knowledge. Unlike $p$-values, posterior probabilities are directly interpretable and provide a more intuitive framework for assessing evidence of association. In the context of epistasis, the Bayesian approach provides a natural way to mitigate the multiple testing problem while identifying subtle, biologically meaningful interactions that may contribute to disease risk\cite{walakiraDetectingGeneGene2022}.
\par
GWAI studies have been relatively understudied in T1D and the methods for epistasis detection continue to evolve along with computational ability. We extend the work of \textit{Walakira et al.}\cite{walakiraDetectingGeneGene2022} and apply this extended method to IA, with the goal of elucidating the epistatic landscape that may shape the early progression of T1D.

